A||||2||mshill||My warm start experiences...||||11||
Z||000000||mshill||03-23-2000||01:23 PM||||Ok, the last couple of days my 86SE V6 (with fresh motor) has been suffering from the warm start bug that has been discussed extensively in the past.<P>In addition to warm starts, it has just plain died twice.  It seems to definitely electrical in nature, and related to heat.  The last time it happened I was accelerating hard up a steep hill in second gear and boom, all the dash lights go on and power gone.<P>I pulled over to the curb and tried to restart.  <P>It cranks but no go.  <BR>Tried clear flood mode (pedal all the way down) no go.<BR>Disconnected tach filter, no go.<BR>Disconnected MAP, no go.<BR>Reconnected tach filter and MAP sensor, still no go.<BR>Disconnected TPS, it started immediately but was stumbling.<BR>Turned off.<BR>Reconnected TPS, it started immediately and was running normal.<P>From this last experience I gathered that it is not the tach filter, MAP or a flooding condition.  <P>I am not sure whether it is the TPS or whether by the time I got to diconnecting the TPS the engine had cooled enough that the offending component had recovered.<P>I know if the TPS goes open circuit you get into the "limp home" mode, but what happens if the TPS gets shorted (5V present at ECM input)?  Does the ECM shut down? <P>||||reg||11
Z||000001||mwbackus||03-23-2000||01:34 PM||||I think these are two different unrelated problems you are observing.  The "engine dying under load while hot" would indicate a weak ignition component: module or pickup coil.  The warm starting problem is usually a defective oil pressure switch, or even just a wornout engine that takes a while longer to build enough oil pressure for the oil pressure switch to kick the fuel pump on.<BR>Fuel injector or cold start valve leaks have also been known to cause some problems with warm starts.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000002||mshill||03-23-2000||03:50 PM||||mwbackus, the pickup coil and ignition module are both ACDelco replacements less than 5 months old.  I also thought the oil pressure switch but while cranking the guage goes up and the oil light goes out (I believe the switch controls the light as well as the fuel pump).  I have ruled out the flooded engine since the clear flood mode failed to get it started.||||reg||11
